  "content": "<p><strong>Release:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/llm-gemini/releases/tag/0.38\">llm-gemini 0.38</a></p>\n        <p>Today Google <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-neon/\">introduced Gemini 4 Neon</a>. I <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Nov/12/llm-gemini/\">wrote recently</a> about how confusing the pricing for the Gemini 4 series was - Neon has somehow made this even worse.</p>\n<p>Previously the Gemini 4 lineup was Hydrogen, Argon, and Carbon - where Argon and Carbon were <em>the same price</em>, and Hydrogen was 3/4 of that price now but will be 1/4 of that price when the other two double an 1st December once their initial discount pricing expires.</p>\n<p>The Neon announcement pretends that Argon doesn't exist. Here's their new image explaining the model line-up:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Comparison table slide titled &quot;Gemini 4 - Our next era of frontier intelligence&quot; with three model columns, Carbon, Neon and Hydrogen, where the Neon column is highlighted with a blue outline. Best For: Carbon - Hardest long-horizon projects and deep research; Neon - Deep reasoning across software engineering, knowledge work, and science; Hydrogen - Speed and efficiency in everyday coding and agentic workflows. Quality (Artificial Analysis Index): Carbon 60, Neon 61, Hydrogen 54. Price ($/1M In / Out (1M flat)): Carbon $4 / $20*, Neon $3 / $15, Hydrogen $1.50 / $7.50. Thinking levels: Carbon - Low, medium,",
